From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: CPU OPPs -- per-bin frequency sets

What. opp-supported-hw on every point of opp-table-cluster0 and
opp-table-cluster1 now names the speed bins that run that frequency,
instead of 0x31 (worst case, bin 4, bin 5) on every point. Nothing else
in the tables changes: no opp-hz, no opp-microvolt line.

Why. Every die the driver cannot identify got index 0, and every point
carried BIT(0), so an unknown die ran the whole ladder -- up to 1794 MHz
on the A55 and 2002 MHz on the A76, with the unnamed worst-case
voltages. The vendor runs no unidentified die that high: its fallback
column vf0100 ends at 1612 / 1794 MHz, and vf0300 and vf0600 end at
1404 / 1612 MHz. The voltages were safe (the worst case is the maximum
over all columns, inside VDD-CPUB/VDD-CPUL 0.8..1.05 V, datasheet V1.01
table 5-2); the clocks were not.

Source. The UM V1.00 and the datasheet V1.01 have no speed-bin or
per-bin frequency table; the datasheet says "up to 2.0 GHz" only. The
per-bin frequency set exists only in the vendor's
sun60iw2p1-cpu-vf.dtsi (Allwinner BSP cubie-aiot-v1.5.0, sha256
d5535630db3d0acdeace832d1dfad684fab9ab0c31c482b493e36a7f3387e5ad; the
CPU columns are unchanged since cubie-aiot-v1.4.8). It is taken as a
restriction only: bit b is set where vf_mapping_table maps fuse value b
to a column that has a voltage at this frequency, 26 MHz family (the
CPU PLLs are DCXO-fed). No voltage comes from it.
The masks are generated by a script whose --check mode verifies
a DTS against the vendor file.

Where the vendor driver differs, and what this patch does:
  - fuse 0x00 selects vf0000, which clocks higher than vf0100 (1794 /
    1976 MHz). A zero fuse cannot be told from an unprogrammed one, so
    we do not carry bit 0; the driver gives such a die the vf0100 set.
    The DSU table leaves vf0000 out for the same reason.
  - fuse 0x11 and 0x12 map to vf0101 and vf0201, which the CPU tables
    do not have. Not carried; the driver treats them as unknown.
  - the vendor runs every bin without a measured column at its own
    voltage; we run it at the worst case.
  - 247 and 494 MHz are ours, not the vendor's. Each gets bit b where
    the vendor runs bin b at some higher point at no more than the
    800 mV these points request: every bin runs 416 and 1014 MHz at
    800 mV, so both carry the full set.

The resulting sets (bits 1-6 = 0x7e, 0x14/0x15 = bits 20/21):
  A55  247..1404 0x0030007e  1508, 1612 0x00300036  1716, 1794 0x00300034
  A76  247..1612 0x0030007e  1716, 1794 0x00300036  1898, 2002 0x00300034
       1950 0x00300030
  Bin 1 (the fallback) therefore ends at 1612 / 1794 MHz, bins 3 and 6
  at 1404 / 1612 MHz, exactly the vendor's ends.

Bins 4 and 5 are unchanged: 0x30 is in every mask, so every point they
had stays, at the measured voltages of opp-microvolt-speed4/5.

The DSU. arm64-dts-a733-let-the-dsu-follow-the-cpu-policies selects a
DSU column only for a bin the CPU tables describe too. With bits 1-6,
20, 21 described here, dies of those bins select their DSU columns
instead of the DSU's worst case -- the behaviour that driver was built
for. Those DSU columns are the vendor's and unproven: only bins 4 and 5
are proven on silicon for the DSU, so a die of another bin runs
untested DSU voltages (still capped by the A55's vote on the shared
rail). A die on the CPU fallback (index 1) keeps the DSU at its own
worst case (index 0); both are the unnamed worst-case column, so the
voltages agree.

Comments corrected in the same place: 494 MHz is not below the vendor's
floor (416 MHz), and not every bin runs 1196 MHz at 800 mV (the A55
worst case there is 850 mV).

Verified: applies at fuzz 0 after the full series; the dtb builds
(compile-check, 6.18.54, native arm64); the script's --check passes on
the result and fails on the tree before this patch (25 points differ);
an independent review re-derived every mask from the vendor file.
Measured on the 8 GiB board, 2026-09-29, with a test-only fuse-word
override (not part of this series): the A55 and A76 policies end at
1612 / 1794 MHz for an unknown fuse word and at 1404 / 1612 MHz for
bin 3, read at the CPU PLL register; bin 5 unchanged without the override.

Assisted-by: Claude
